      "math_academy_id": "q-78178",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Given that  $({t}^{2}-6t+9)({t}^{2}-4t+4)={t}^{4}+a{t}^{3}+b{t}^{2}+ct+36,$ calculate $a+b+c.$",
      "worked_solution": "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}-6t+9)({t}^{2}-4t+4) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}({t}^{2}-4t+4)+(-6t)({t}^{2}-4t+4)+9({t}^{2}-4t+4) & = \\\\ {t}^{4}-4{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}-6{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}-24t+9{t}^{2}-36t+36 & = \\\\ {t}^{4}-10{t}^{3}+37{t}^{2}-60t+36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-10,$ $b=37,$ and $c=-60.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-10+37+(-60)=-33.\n$$",
